{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Cut the overlap step's token cost: hoist the shared query digest into the cacheable system prefix\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`rebar review-plan` spends the majority of its input tokens in the Stage-2 overlap judge. Measured\non this repo against the real API after bug `afeb` landed (so `overlap-judge` already runs on the\n`standard` class, `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`): 12 judge calls over a 6-candidate corpus of real\n`rebar.llm.enrich` digests billed **16,040 input tokens**, with `cache_read_tokens: 0` and\n`cache_write_tokens: 0` on every single call. A recording proxy runner around\n`overlap.judge.judge()` showed the query ticket's digest block (308 tokens) present verbatim in the\nuser message of **12 of 12** calls, while the system prompt was byte-identical across all 12 (377\ntokens). The repeated content is paid for at full price on every call because it sits in the\nvolatile user block, which is never a cache prefix.\n\n## Approach\n\nHoist the ONE digest that is invariant across a batch — the query ticket's — out of the per-call\nuser message and into the system prompt, so the bytes shared by every call in the batch sit inside\nthe block that already carries a cache breakpoint. This is the operator's chosen design and it fits\nbecause the engine already applies `anthropic_cache_instructions=True` to the system block at the\nshared runner seam (`llm/capabilities.py:cache_settings_for`, dispatched in\n`llm/structured_run.py`), so no new caching machinery is introduced: the only reason today's calls\ncache nothing is that the stable block is too small. It is also the same stable-prefix / volatile-\nbody split the engine already uses for workflow steps via `prompts.resolve_prompt_cached`\n(`llm/prompting/prompts.py:438`), so it follows an established in-repo shape.\n\n`judge()` judges each candidate in BOTH orderings — `(query, cand)` and `(cand, query)` — so the\nquery digest is FIRST in one and SECOND in the other. The relocation must preserve that ordered-pair\nreading, on which the prompt's directional semantics (\"FIRST <relation> SECOND\") depend. It does so\nby keeping the FIRST/SECOND scaffold in the user message and putting a fixed reference in the slot\nthe hoisted digest occupies:\n\n    system:  <overlap-judge prompt>\n             QUERY TICKET DIGEST — <one-line orientation>\n             component_or_area: ... (the query digest block)\n\n    user (ordering 1):  FIRST:\n                        the QUERY TICKET DIGEST given in your system prompt\n\n                        SECOND:\n                        component_or_area: ... (the candidate digest block)\n\n    user (ordering 2):  FIRST:\n                        component_or_area: ... (the candidate digest block)\n\n                        SECOND:\n                        the QUERY TICKET DIGEST given in your system prompt\n\nBecause the system block carries the same query digest in both orderings, it is byte-identical\nacross all 2N calls of a batch — one cache write on the first call, reads on the remaining 2N-1.\n\n**Riskiest assumption, tested first and already falsified in its dangerous direction.** The plan\nrests on the prefix being large enough to cache at all. `llm/capabilities.py:330` hardcodes\n`CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS = 4096` and documents it as the *Opus 4.8* floor; a 4096 floor would make\nthis design write and read nothing. A direct two-call probe against `claude-sonnet-4-6` at\nincreasing system sizes measured the real floor at **1024** (922 tokens: write 0 / read 0; 1042\ntokens: write 1035 then read 1035). The assumption survives, but only just: the post-move prefix\nmeasures ~1,024 tokens (377 system + 308 digest + ~339 of structured-output tool surface ahead of\nthe breakpoint), i.e. at the boundary rather than safely past it. The relocation is therefore\n**never worse** — identical bytes, moved from an unbillable-as-prefix block to a cacheable one — and\nits saving is conditional on the reviewed ticket's digest clearing the floor. This plan commits to\nreporting the measured after-numbers honestly, including a zero saving if that is the result, rather\nthan asserting a win.\n\nDeliberately NOT done here: the loop is not parallelized. `judge()` is a sequential\n`for cand in candidates:`, so the first call warms the prefix and the rest read it. Fanning out cold\ncalls that share a prefix would turn one cache write plus N-1 cheap reads into N premium writes —\nstrictly more expensive than today — so parallelism stays out until it can be sequenced behind a\ncompleted warm-up call.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/overlap/judge.py` — the only production change. `judge_one` gains one keyword-only\n  argument naming which side of the pair is the hoisted shared digest; a module-private helper\n  builds the augmented system prompt and another builds the FIRST/SECOND user block with the\n  reference substituted into the hoisted slot. `judge()` passes `\"first\"` for ordering 1 and\n  `\"second\"` for ordering 2. Default (argument omitted) reproduces today's exact request bytes, so\n  no other caller changes.\n- `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py` — new tests below, added to the existing file.\n\nNo other file changes. `src/rebar/llm/overlap/retrieve.py` and `src/rebar/llm/overlap/wire.py` were\ndeclared on this ticket while options 1/3 were still open (tightening BM25F retrieval, and the\nadvisory fail-safe); the chosen design touches neither, and the fail-safe in `wire.py` and\n`judge_one`'s `except` are preserved unchanged rather than edited.\n\n## Testing\n\nAll tests are offline and drive the real `judge()` through a recording fake runner that captures\neach `RunRequest`, so they assert on the ACTUAL request payload rather than on a proxy.\n\n- **Happy path — the shared digest is hoisted and not duplicated.** Drive `judge()` with one query\n  digest and two candidate digests (4 calls). Assert: every captured `system_prompt` is byte-\n  identical; the query digest block appears in the system prompt; the query digest block appears in\n  ZERO of the 4 `instructions`; each candidate's digest block appears in its own `instructions`.\n- **Ordered-pair semantics survive the hoist.** For the same run, assert ordering 1's `instructions`\n  places the shared-digest reference in the FIRST slot and the candidate block in the SECOND slot,\n  and ordering 2 places them the other way round — i.e. the text between `FIRST:` and `SECOND:` is\n  the reference for one call of a pair and the candidate block for the other.\n- **Failure/edge path — the advisory fail-safe is unchanged.** A runner that raises on every call\n  still yields `judge(...) == []` with no exception escaping, and a candidate whose digest is\n  missing from the corpus is still skipped without a call.\n- **Back-compat edge.** `judge_one` called without the new argument produces `system_prompt` and\n  `instructions` byte-identical to today's shape (`FIRST:\\n<block>\\n\\nSECOND:\\n<block>` with the\n  unaugmented prompt) — the default path is untouched.\n- **Mutation check, both directions, for every new test:** revert `judge.py` and confirm each new\n  test FAILS; restore and confirm it passes. Recorded on this ticket.\n- Full gate run: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and\n  `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 6 --dist worksteal -q`, judged by exit code.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The query digest is sent in the system prompt and nowhere else: a recording-runner test over\n      `judge()` shows the query digest block in a byte-identical `system_prompt` on every call and\n      in ZERO `instructions`; checked by the new test in `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py`.\n- [ ] Ordered-pair semantics are preserved: a test asserts the shared-digest reference occupies the\n      FIRST slot in ordering 1 and the SECOND slot in ordering 2, with the candidate block in the\n      other slot; checked by the new ordering test.\n- [ ] The step stays advisory and fail-safe: with a runner that raises on every call, `judge()`\n      returns `[]` and raises nothing; checked by the existing/extended error test.\n- [ ] The default path is byte-identical: `judge_one` without the new keyword argument produces the\n      same `system_prompt` and `instructions` as before the change; checked by the back-compat test.\n- [ ] A BEFORE/AFTER measurement from real `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` calls over the SAME query\n      ticket and the SAME candidate digest corpus is recorded as a ticket comment, reporting total\n      `input_tokens`, `cache_read_tokens` AND `cache_write_tokens` for both runs. The AFTER run must\n      not show more `cache_write_tokens` than one prefix write; a design that turns one write into N\n      writes fails this criterion. A zero or negative saving is a valid, reportable result and must\n      be recorded as such with the measured prefix size and the measured 1024 floor beside it.\n- [ ] Every new test is mutation-checked in both directions (fails with `judge.py` reverted, passes\n      with it restored) and the evidence is recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the non-integration pytest run all exit 0."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785706544674505001,"uuid":"a2e8388a-7315-44a6-8c62-8df8a827ab1c"}